---
// Museum Extras page — modern companion tools, hardware, and legal/preservation notes.
// Content sourced from src/content/docs/rebirth-website-structure-plan.md §2.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import HardwarePanel from '../components/ui/HardwarePanel.astro';
import ExternalLink from '../components/ExternalLink.astro';
import ArchiveLink from '../components/ArchiveLink.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Extras"
  description="Modern companion tools, hardware tributes, and preservation notes for ReBirth RB-338 — ReMaker, the LMNC hardware controller, ReBirth for iPad, and where to legally get the software."
>
  <div class="extras-page">
    <div class="container">
      <Breadcrumbs
        items={[
          { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
          { label: 'Extras', href: '/extras' },
        ]}
      />

      <HardwarePanel
        variant="rackmount-chassis"
        animate
        title="MUSEUM EXTRAS"
        ledStatus="online"
        ledLabel="COMPANION TOOLS"
        class="rb-panel--center-header rb-panel--padded-body"
      >
        <p class="extras-subtitle">
          Modern tools, hardware tributes, and legacy apps built around ReBirth RB-338 — plus where
          the original software can still be legally obtained.
        </p>
      </HardwarePanel>

      <!-- ReMaker vs in-browser MIDI export -->
      <HardwarePanel
        variant="rackmount"
        animate
        title="REMAKER 1.32 — MIDI EXTRACTION"
        ledStatus="online"
        ledLabel="SECTION 01"
      >
        <div class="extras-prose">
          <p>
            <strong>ReMaker</strong> is a standalone Windows utility, now mirrored by <ExternalLink
              href="https://nordbeat.com/remaker"
              kind="external"
              host="nordbeat.com"
            /> and archived on <ExternalLink
              href="https://archive.org/details/rebirth-2.01-2001-version"
              kind="external"
              host="archive.org"
            />, that converts ReBirth 2.0 <code>.rbs</code> songs into Standard MIDI-1 Format (SMF) —
            and back again. It reads the same pattern, arrangement, and note data this archive's player
            parses, but it runs against a real ReBirth 2.0 install rather than a from-scratch parser,
            so it only handles 2.0-generation songs; a 1.5 file has to be reopened and re-saved in ReBirth
            2.0 first before ReMaker will touch it.
          </p>
          <h3>What this site's own MIDI export does differently</h3>
          <p>
            Every playable song in this archive can export a Standard MIDI File directly in the
            browser — no Windows install, no emulator. Open a song in the <ArchiveLink
              href="/archive/songs">archive player</ArchiveLink
            > and use the <strong>MIDI</strong> button to export patterns and arrangement order for both
            v1/v1.5 (MIDI-container) and v2 (<code>CAT </code>/<code>RB40</code>) songs alike,
            straight from the in-browser WASM parser. It does not (yet) carry ReMaker's round-trip —
            MIDI-to-<code>.rbs</code> — or export the automation curves ReMaker can read from a live ReBirth
            session; those stay a reason to keep ReMaker around for deep archival work.
          </p>
        </div>
      </HardwarePanel>

      <!-- Hardware / modern twist -->
      <HardwarePanel
        variant="rackmount-module"
        animate
        title="HARDWARE & MODERN TRIBUTES"
        ledStatus="online"
        ledLabel="SECTION 02"
      >
        <div class="extras-prose">
          <h3>The LMNC ReBirth-338 Controller</h3>
          <p>
            In 2023, <strong>Sam Battle (Look Mum No Computer)</strong> built a dedicated hardware MIDI
            controller for ReBirth RB-338 — a box covered in switches and potentiometers wired to drive
            the emulator's twin TB-303s and drum machines like a real acid machine, turning a piece of
            1990s abandonware into a tactile instrument again. Watch the build on <ExternalLink
              href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QRyP5YrfsIk"
              kind="external"
              host="youtube.com"
            />.
          </p>
          <h3>ReBirth for iPad</h3>
          <p>
            Propellerhead ported ReBirth to iPhone and iPad in 2010 (with Retronyms), giving the
            13-year-old engine a second life on touchscreens. Roland's 2017 IP claim over the
            emulated TB-303/TR-808/TR-909 forced Propellerhead to pull it from the App Store that
            June — the same claim that keeps the desktop version from ever being resold today. A
            copy of the discontinued app is preserved on <ExternalLink
              href="https://archive.org/details/rebirth-app"
              kind="external"
              host="archive.org"
            />.
          </p>
          <h3>Legacy Community Hubs</h3>
          <p>
            <ExternalLink
              href="https://nordbeat.com/rebirth-corner"
              kind="external"
              host="nordbeat.com"
            />
            {' '}mirrors ReBirth Corner, once a small fan hub for sounds, mods, and inspiration from
            the software's late-90s/early-2000s heyday. <ExternalLink
              href="https://www.peff.com/rebirth/"
              kind="external"
              host="peff.com"
            /> — Kurt "Peff" Kurasaki's long-running archive — remains the primary curated source this
            site's own mod and song mirrors are built from.
          </p>
        </div>
      </HardwarePanel>

      <!-- Legal / abandonware notice -->
      <HardwarePanel
        variant="rackmount"
        animate
        title="GETTING REBIRTH ITSELF — LEGAL & PRESERVATION NOTES"
        ledStatus="online"
        ledLabel="SECTION 03"
      >
        <div class="extras-prose">
          <p>
            Propellerhead discontinued ReBirth RB-338 in September 2005 and released it as a free
            download rather than sunset it entirely. This archive preserves community
            <code>.rbs</code> songs and <code>.rbm</code> mods and plays them back through an original
            in-browser engine — <strong
              >it does not host, mirror, or redistribute the Propellerhead installer or ISO itself.</strong
            >
          </p>
          <p>
            The last official 2.0.1 build is preserved on the Internet Archive, including a
            community-repackaged installer for modern 64-bit Windows: <ExternalLink
              href="https://archive.org/details/rebirthrb338forwin7810"
              kind="external"
              host="archive.org"
            />. Running it on current hardware still generally means a compatibility layer — Wine on
            Linux, an emulated Windows 9x/Mac OS 9 environment (QEMU, SheepShaver), or one of the
            patched Windows 10/11 installers linked from that Archive.org page — since the original
            executable predates modern OS security and driver models. See the <ArchiveLink
              href="/history">History page</ArchiveLink
            > for more on the emulation path.
          </p>
          <p class="extras-callout">
            These are community/fan-preserved archives. Respect original copyrights, and treat
            third-party installers as unofficial redistributions of abandoned software, not current
            Propellerhead/Reason Studios releases.
          </p>
        </div>
      </HardwarePanel>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .extras-page {
    padding-block: 2rem 4rem;
    background: #0f0f0f;
    min-height: 60vh;
  }

  .extras-subtitle {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: #888;
    margin: 0;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .extras-prose {
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: #aaa;
  }

  .extras-prose p {
    margin-block: 0 1em;
  }

  .extras-prose h3 {
    font-family: var(--rb-font-display, 'Courier New', monospace);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.15em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--rb-amber);
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #2a2a2a;
    padding-bottom: 0.3rem;
  }

  .extras-prose h3:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .extras-prose strong {
    color: var(--rb-silver);
  }

  .extras-prose code {
    background: rgba(255, 176, 0, 0.08);
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 176, 0, 0.2);
    border-radius: 3px;
    padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
    font-size: 0.85em;
    color: var(--rb-amber);
  }

  .extras-callout {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--rb-amber-dim, #b37800);
    padding-left: 1rem;
    color: #999;
    font-style: italic;
  }
</style>
